\details{
With \code{variables = NULL}, this function averages patients' SOPs using their
observed covariates, returning one estimate per time and state. Supply \code{by}
to average separately within groups.

Supplying \code{variables} implements G-computation (standardization):
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Counterfactual Creation}: For each value in \code{variables}, creates a
copy of \code{newdata} with that variable set to the specified value.
\item \strong{Individual Prediction}: Calls \code{sops()} to compute individual-level
SOPs for each patient under each counterfactual scenario.
\item \strong{Marginalization}: Averages individual SOPs across patients within
each time-state-treatment combination, yielding population-average
(marginal) probabilities.
}

With \code{variables = list(tx = c(0, 1))}, the result represents the expected SOP
if the entire population received treatment or control, averaged over the
observed covariate distribution.
When \code{newdata} is omitted, the average uses the starting profiles stored by
\code{\link[=mostm]{mostm()}}: exactly the fitted patients with a complete designated starting
profile and
at least one usable likelihood transition. A missing response at the
designated row alone does not exclude a patient who contributes a later
transition. Patients with no usable fitted transition are excluded, and a
fitted patient without a complete designated profile is an error.

The average gives every profile the same weight. Case weights given to
\code{\link[=mostm]{mostm()}} with \code{weights} are used only to fit the model, not in this
average, so the result is not the same as repeating each patient according
to their weight.

For \code{rmsb::blrm()} models, patient-level posterior arrays are chunked and
marginalized by draw before summarizing to reduce memory use.
State-wise medians and interval bounds are not constrained to sum to one
across states; use \code{posterior_summary = "mean"} when the displayed estimates
themselves need to preserve total probability. Draw-level probabilities
stored with \code{return_draws = TRUE} remain normalized within each draw.

For supported frequentist models, \code{inferences(method = "delta")} adds
analytical confidence intervals. Its default \code{vcov = "unconditional"}
accounts for coefficient estimation and sampling the patients used in the
average. Use \code{vcov = "conditional"} to treat those patients' starting states
and covariates as given and account only for coefficient estimation.
User-supplied \code{newdata}, and models fitted with case weights, require the
conditional choice. See \code{\link[=inferences]{inferences()}} for model restrictions.
